## React Re-renders: Causes and Prevention

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props or state change. For primitives, "change" means a different value. For objects and functions, it means a different **reference** — even if the contents are identical.

```jsx
// Re-renders Child every time Parent renders, because {} !== {} by reference
function Parent() {
  return <Child style={{ color: 'red' }} onClick={() => doSomething()} />;
}
```

Every render of `Parent` creates a **new object literal** and a **new function**, so `Child` always sees "new" props.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

| Tool | Memoizes | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `React.memo` | A component's render output | Child is pure and re-renders are expensive |
| `useMemo` | A computed value | Computation is expensive or result is passed as a prop |
| `useCallback` | A function reference | Function is passed as a prop to a memoized child |

**`React.memo`** — skips re-render if props are shallowly equal:
```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ onClick, style }) { ... });
```

**`useCallback`** — stabilizes a function reference across renders:
```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(), []);
```

**`useMemo`** — stabilizes an object/computed value reference:
```jsx
const style = useMemo(() => ({ color: 'red' }), []);
```

**When NOT to use them:** These are optimizations with a cost (memory + cognitive overhead). Don't reach for them preemptively — profile first, then memoize. A component that renders fast doesn't need `memo`.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Context triggers a re-render in **every consumer** when the context value changes reference. A common mistake:

```jsx
// Bad: new object on every render → all consumers re-render
function AuthProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </AuthContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

**Fixes:**

```jsx
// Fix 1: memoize the value
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
return <AuthContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</AuthContext.Provider>;

// Fix 2 (better for large apps): split stable and volatile context
// UserDispatchContext never changes → subscribers don't re-render on user updates
const UserStateContext = createContext(null);    // changes with user
const UserDispatchContext = createContext(null); // stable setUser ref
```

---

### 4. Before / After Example

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every keystroke in the parent:

```jsx
function SearchPage() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items, setItems] = useState(initialItems);

  const filtered = items.filter(i => i.name.includes(query)); // new array each render
  const handleDelete = (id) => setItems(prev => prev.filter(i => i.id !== id)); // new fn

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onDelete={handleDelete} />
    </>
  );
}

function ExpensiveList({ items, onDelete }) { /* renders a huge list */ }
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `items` content or `query` actually changes:

```jsx
function SearchPage() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items, setItems] = useState(initialItems);

  // stable reference unless items/query change
  const filtered = useMemo(
    () => items.filter(i => i.name.includes(query)),
    [items, query]
  );

  // stable reference unless setItems changes (it never does)
  const handleDelete = useCallback(
    (id) => setItems(prev => prev.filter(i => i.id !== id)),
    []
  );

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onDelete={handleDelete} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ items, onDelete }) {
  /* now skips re-render when query changes but filter result is identical */
});
```

**The fix requires all three pieces working together:** `React.memo` on the child, `useMemo` for the derived array, and `useCallback` for the handler — any one missing breaks the optimization since a single unstable prop defeats `memo`.
